<h1>Privacy</h1>
<main class="authText">
	<div class="authScroll">
	<p><b>Project privacy policy:</b><br><a href="<?= htmlspecialchars($privacy, ENT_QUOTES) ?>" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><?= htmlspecialchars($privacy, ENT_QUOTES) ?></a></p>
	<p>This notice covers the standard functions of the systopic framework and fluxo cms: signing in, managing your account and editing content in the administration interface. It does not cover visitors to the public website or additional project-specific integrations.</p>
	<p>The controller is the operator named in the <a href="<?= htmlspecialchars($imprint, ENT_QUOTES) ?>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">imprint</a>. The operator's contact details, any appointed data protection officer, hosting arrangements and supplementary privacy information are available in the <a href="<?= htmlspecialchars($privacy, ENT_QUOTES) ?>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">operator's privacy policy</a>. systopic GmbH supplies the software; that alone does not make it the controller or a recipient of this installation's account data.</p>
	<p>This notice describes the software defaults; the linked operator information must identify the actual controller, applicable legal bases, providers and retention rules of this installation. It is information about processing, not a request for consent. Signing in does not waive your data protection rights.</p>
	<h2>Accounts and authentication</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The system processes your account name, email address, profile information and permissions. Changes to content can be associated with the account that created or last edited it.</li>
		<li>Passwords are checked during sign-in; the account stores a password hash rather than the password as entered.</li>
		<li>If you add a passkey, the account stores its credential identifier, public key, chosen name, usage counter and timestamps. The private key and any fingerprint or face data used to unlock it remain with your device or passkey provider and are not sent to the CMS.</li>
		<li>Invitation, recovery and remote-login codes are checked using stored hashes. Remote login also processes the account and device being connected and the account that approves it. QR scanning uses the camera locally in the browser; the standard scanner does not upload or store camera frames.</li>
		<li>Invitation, password recovery and account-security emails use your email address and relevant account information through the mail service configured by the operator. Passwords and private passkey keys are not included.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Cookies and browser storage</h2>
	<p>The following storage supports authentication, the current editing session and interface settings. It is not used by the standard CMS for advertising or cross-site audience measurement.</p>
	<ul>
		<li><b>Session cookie:</b> links requests to the current session. Its name and lifetime depend on this installation's PHP/session configuration; it is normally a browser-session cookie. Server-side session expiry can end access earlier.</li>
		<li><b>Device cookie:</b> contains a device identifier and random secret to recognise a signed-in device. The browser cookie is renewed with a maximum lifetime of <?= (int) Devices::COOKIE_DAYS ?> days. This does not guarantee access for that time: person devices expire after <?= (int) $deviceDays ?> days without use in this installation. <?php if ($screenDays === null) { ?>Screen accounts remain authorised until revoked or otherwise disabled.<?php } else { ?>Screen devices expire after <?= (int) $screenDays ?> days without use.<?php } ?> Signing out or revoking a device invalidates its access. Device records contain a device name, browser/user-agent details, client mode, timestamps and the last IP address.</li>
		<li><b>Remote-login cookie (<code>sys_pair</code>):</b> holds a random polling secret for the temporary pairing process. It expires with that process, normally after ten minutes. <code>sys_plain</code> is a temporary display flag, valid for up to ten minutes or removed on the next normal CMS visit.</li>
		<li><b>Display cookies:</b> <code>cmsTimezone</code> stores the browser's time zone for date display, and <code>cmsMobile</code> stores the compact-layout flag, each for up to one year and renewed when set again. Where supplied by the client, <code>sys_display</code> reports whether the interface runs in a browser or installed-app mode.</li>
		<li><b>Local Storage:</b> <code>cmsDevice</code>, <code>cmsDeviceMobile</code> and <code>cmsSidebarClosed</code> remember your selected preview widths and collapsed property sections. They have no automatic expiry and remain until overwritten or the site's browser data are cleared.</li>
		<li><b>Session Storage:</b> <code>cmsDoc</code> and <code>cmsOpenSheet</code> preserve mobile window navigation within the browser tab. The pending navigation value is consumed when used; session storage is normally removed when the tab's session ends.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>You can clear these entries in your browser's site-data settings. Clearing authentication cookies can sign you out, and blocking them can prevent sign-in. The operator must distinguish storage strictly necessary for a service you request from optional convenience features; mentioning a feature here does not replace any consent required under § 25 TDDDG.</p>
	<h2>Login log and security</h2>
	<p>Authentication events, including sign-ins, failed attempts, sign-outs, invitations and remote approvals, are logged with the time, account name or email address, IP address, action and sign-in method where available. An event can also identify the approving account or invitation recipient. Authorised administrators can access the log to investigate misuse and account-access problems. Failed attempts can be counted by IP address to limit guessing.</p>
	<p>The standard system does not automatically delete login-log entries after a fixed number of days. Retention depends on the operator's documented need to detect and investigate unauthorised access, resolve authentication incidents and establish relevant account activity. The assessment must consider the detection and investigation window, whether an incident remains unresolved, the sensitivity and volume of the information and whether less identifying information would serve the same purpose. Storage capacity, a possible future use or the absence of a fixed statutory deadline alone do not justify continued retention.</p>
	<p>The operator must define and document review intervals and, where determinable, deletion periods for this installation and make the applicable rules available in the linked operator information. When the relevant purpose ends, entries must be deleted or effectively anonymised without undue delay unless a specific legal obligation or another lawful, necessary purpose requires retention. Only records relevant to a concrete incident, statutory obligation or the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims may be retained for that additional purpose, with restricted access and a documented end or review criterion. This does not justify keeping the entire log for the longest conceivable claim period.</p>
	<p>Temporary session-name markers help attribute an expired session. The existing cleanup removes markers older than <?= (int) LoginLog::KEEP_DAYS ?> days when a new session is created; it is not a scheduled daily deletion, so physical removal can occur later. This technical cleanup threshold does not determine retention of the login log itself.</p>
	<h2>External services inside the CMS</h2>
	<p>A direct browser request to another provider necessarily exposes your IP address and technical request information, such as browser details and, depending on the browser's referrer policy, the referring origin or page. The CMS does not deliberately add your account password or private passkey key to these requests. Providers can nevertheless associate requests with other information, for example an existing provider login.</p>
	<ul>
		<li><b>Generated portraits — DiceBear:</b> if an account has no uploaded photo, the browser loads an avatar from <code>api.dicebear.com</code>, operated by Florian Körner, Germany. For a person, the current implementation sends an MD5 checksum of the trimmed, lower-case email address, or an account identifier where no email is present, plus theme colours. A screen avatar uses a checksum derived from the account identifier. The seed selects a repeatable image; it is not an authentication secret or a guarantee of anonymity. An email checksum can be matched against candidate email addresses and must not be treated as anonymous merely because the original address is not included. The seed is used for image selection, not identity verification. Uploaded profile photos are served by this installation. See <a href="https://www.dicebear.com/legal/privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DiceBear's privacy information</a>.</li>
		<li><b>Language flags — Flagcdn:</b> language controls load flag images from <code>flagcdn.com</code>, a Flagpedia.net service operated by David Krmela, Czech Republic, using Cloudflare for delivery. The image URL contains the selected flag code, rather than a CMS account name or email address. Network request data are still transmitted. See <a href="https://flagpedia.net/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flagpedia's privacy information</a>.</li>
		<li><b>YouTube and Vimeo in the media editor:</b> when an external video is opened for editing, the standard file editor embeds its player directly. The request can start when the editor opens, before playback. YouTube uses <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>; Vimeo uses <code>player.vimeo.com</code> with <code>dnt=1</code>. These options do not prevent network requests or guarantee that no personal data or device information are processed. The provider receives the video identifier and request information and can process playback interactions. See <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google/YouTube's privacy information</a> and <a href="https://vimeo.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vimeo's privacy information</a>. This description concerns the CMS editor, not the public website's player or consent settings.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>These services and their delivery networks may process data outside the EU/EEA. The operator must determine the applicable legal basis and any required consent before enabling them, and identify the actual recipients and applicable transfer safeguards for this installation. The supplementary information must state whether an adequacy decision applies or which appropriate safeguards support the transfer and how a copy can be obtained. A provider link or a privacy-enhanced player setting does not itself establish those safeguards or collect consent. Where prior consent is required, requests must be prevented until it has been given; this notice does not implement such a block. Additional requests made by project templates shown in the CMS preview require supplementary information from the operator.</p>
	<h2>Purposes, legal bases and recipients</h2>
	<p>Account and authentication processing provides authorised access and the editing functions requested by you or your organisation. Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR applies where processing is necessary to perform a contract with you; Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR may apply to secure operation and preventing misuse, subject to the operator's assessment. A contract with your employer or organisation does not by itself make Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR applicable to your personal data. Employment-related use can require a different or supplementary legal basis determined by the operator. Any statutory obligation relied on under Art. 6 (1) (c) GDPR must be identified by the operator; it is not presumed for all account or log data. Where processing requires consent, the applicable basis is Art. 6 (1) (a) GDPR, and you may withdraw that consent with future effect. The legal basis for external services must be assessed separately from the basis for account security.</p>
	<p>Recipients can include authorised account administrators, the operator's hosting and mail providers, and the external services described above when requested by your browser. Access by service providers must be limited to their agreed tasks. The operator identifies its actual providers, processing locations and any international-transfer arrangements in its supplementary information.</p>
	<h2>Storage and your rights</h2>
	<p>Account and permission data are kept while needed to provide and administer your access; the operator reviews and deletes accounts that are no longer needed. Passkeys and device records are managed with that account, but an expired or revoked device is not necessarily deleted immediately. Content and its editing attribution follow the operator's content-retention rules. The login-log retention criteria and browser-storage lifetimes are stated above. The operator specifies retention for mail, server logs and backups separately. Backup copies subject to a justified recovery cycle must be protected against ordinary use, expire under a defined schedule and have applicable deletion or restriction decisions reapplied after restoration. A backup is not an exception permitting unlimited retention. There is no general permission to keep personal data indefinitely merely because storage is available.</p>
	<p>Subject to the applicable conditions, you can ask the operator for access, correction, deletion, restriction and data portability. Where processing relies on Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR, you may object on grounds relating to your particular situation. The operator must then stop that processing unless it demonstrates compelling legitimate grounds overriding your interests, rights and freedoms, or needs the data for legal claims. Where processing relies on consent, you may withdraw it at any time without affecting the lawfulness of earlier processing. Portability applies where the statutory conditions are met, including automated processing based on consent or a contract. You can complain to a competent data protection supervisory authority, in particular where you live or work or where an alleged infringement occurred. The operator's contact details are linked above. An account and a supported way of signing in are necessary for CMS access; without the necessary account and authentication data, that access cannot be provided. Optional profile information and optional sign-in methods are not a general prerequisite. Data can be provided by you, by an administrator who creates or manages your account, or automatically by your browser and authentication device as described above. The standard authentication functions do not make decisions under Art. 22 GDPR producing legal or similarly significant effects.</p>
